A||||12||88Style||Clutch pressure went bydeby||||1||
Z||000000||88Style||11-12-2001||12:08 PM||||Here is a good one for you guys. I do some work, fix some bugs and my 88 is runnun great. Drive home, decided to take the fiero sceptical wife for a drive. Well, it's runnin fine, she is impressed. So what happens next? Peddle starts getting spongey. By the time we get about 2 miles from home, there is no pedal left! <P>I didn't have any forwarning to this at all! Thinking back, in the last few days, it's been hard getting it into first but it's a high milage getrag anyway so I thought it might be loosing a synco. I don't know if this has anything at all to do with the problem though. It was wierd that, aside from the above, the clutch seems to disengage just fine. In anycase, I can't find a leak point yet but there is no fluid in the resivour but there is this black slug at the bottom. <P>I haven't bottomed out the car at all or hit anything that would damage the line running to the slave but it's strange that it seemed to loose pressure all of a sudden. Cracked line maybe but does anyone have any other ideas?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||lou_dias||11-12-2001||12:13 PM||||could be the spring in your master broke<BR>you can tear it apart and replace the spring.<BR>where to find one I don't know, my was replaced with 2 Suzuki springs.  Same diameter, half the length, shifts great now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||88Style||11-12-2001||12:18 PM||||Actually, let me rephrase something. It didn't loose presure instantly, just over the course of a few miles of stoplights. It got rapidly but progressively weaker until there was no hydralics at all. Knowning my luck, it wouldn't be the spring. That would be to easy!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||11-12-2001||12:27 PM||||You have a bad master and/or bad slave cylinder.  Crawl under the dash and remove the rubber boot from the firewall that the rod to the master cylinder runs through.  If there is any fluid at all, then the master is bad.<P>Also, remove the slave cylinder from the bracket (don't remove the hydraulic line) and remove the boot/rod and look for fluid.  Again, if there is any fluid present the slave is bad.<P>When replacing the master & slave only buy/use genuine GM clutch hydraulics.  Don't waste your time with any aftermarket stuff.....you will be worse off than you are now.<P>Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||11-12-2001||12:56 PM||||That happened suddenly to me with a 4 spd. It had the welded up piecemeal clutch yoke and it all fell apart inside the bell housing. Theres a new OEM replacement thats all one piece forged, also have to install different pivot shaft. Just something else to look at.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88Style||11-12-2001||02:29 PM||||It's wierd. I mean the clutch itself is fine but the something is jacked in the hydralics. The slug in the bottom of the master resivour is freaking me out a little bit. Maybe I should just replace the whole damn system with new parts and braided steal line. Kinda like killing a fly with a bazooka but atleast it will work! I want to figure out what went wrong though.||||reg||1
Z||000006||LivoniaFieroGT||11-12-2001||04:48 PM||||I have a new clutch master cylinder and clutch slave (p/n 10079730 & 10038878) for the MG2 getrag, $130 shipped anywhere in US.  Being forced to sell items so spouse can safely walk to freezer in garage.  Also would be nice to see if garage floor is still painted.... ahh...  it is really that bad.... but...<BR>Rob<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||88Style||11-12-2001||04:54 PM||||Well if you spilled any brake fluid on that painted floor, it probably isn't painted anymore! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. YOu have mail!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||lou_dias||11-12-2001||07:15 PM||||Well the spring is very important to pressure.  It is what makes you pedal rise all the way to the top to move all the fluid.  If you can move the pedal up yourself and shift correctly then it's the spring.  don't rule it out especially if your fluid level is fine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||11-12-2001||10:04 PM||||My '85 SE did more or less the same thing a few months ago: clutch got softer over the period of a day or so, then wouldn't completely disengage.  I think the slave cylinder was seeping.  I refilled the reservoir and pumped it a few times; it got better for a couple of months, then failed for good.<P>If you replace the master cylinder, check the <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015204.html" TARGET=_blank>pushrod length</A>.  May not be a problem on an '88, but it was a problem on my '85.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierocritter||11-12-2001||10:21 PM||||It is either the master clylinder, brake line, slave clylinder, throw out bearing or pressure plate. I had an 84 Fiero that did this and it was the pressure plate along with no clutch disc. the bearing was also fried. Hope tha helps. Also have someone push the pedal while you watch the arm on the tranny hooked to the slave clylinder. If it moves when the person pushes in the clutch pedal than your problem is internal. If not than its hydraulic.||||reg||1
Z||000011||88Style||11-13-2001||10:51 AM||||I doubt it's the spring. When I checked the fluid level that night, there was none <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I haven't look very hard but I can't see any blatently obvious fluid leakage but that doesn't mean anything. I have yet to look for it under good light and I haven't been able to get under it. I will check the line because the car did sit for quite a while before I bought it. I think it's probably one of the cylinders. Even if it isn't, the amount of slug in the bottom of the resivour makes me want to replace it anyway. Anyone know a good way to run some Berrymans or some other solvant through the lines to break up any goop or is that a bad idea?||||reg||1
Z||000012||88Style||11-13-2001||03:38 PM||||FYI to anyone that might turn this up in a search, it was my slave cylinder. I didn't see any obvious leaks but when I squeezed the boot, it spewed fluid everywhere!||||reg||1
